# Record 494.
talk(494,'1980-10-27','I. M. Gelfand; A. A. Kirillov intervention','P000924-P000927','Elementary group problems and projective structures on the line',r'''The dated heading is on P000925. The problems concern finite symmetry groups, the topology and group law of the universal cover of SL(2,R), a triple-line cocycle, and Kirillov’s classification of locally projective structures on the real line. The material ends above Gaveau’s heading on P000926. The catalogue’s preceding P000924 still belongs to the 20 October D-module discussion.''')
item(494,'P000925','problem','Subgroups and normal subgroups of finite symmetry groups',r'''Find all subgroups and normal subgroups of symmetry groups of regular polyhedra and of the symmetric group; these are described as problems for first-year students.''',r'''For a specified finite group \(G\), enumerate its subgroups and test normality. For \(S_n\), \(n\ge5\), the normal subgroups are \(1,A_n,S_n\); for \(S_4\), add the normal Klein four group. The small cases must be treated separately. For the Platonic solids one must distinguish rotation groups \(A_4,S_4,A_5\) from full reflection-containing symmetry groups. Enumerating all subgroups of \(S_n\) is a finite problem for each specified \(n\), not a short uniform list.''','E','These are classical finite-group exercises. A complete finite enumeration can start at the trivial subgroup and repeatedly adjoin elements until no new subgroup is obtained; every subgroup is eventually reached by its finite generating list. The source supplies neither a value of n nor the rotation/full-symmetry convention.','the normal-subgroup assertion and finite enumeration are established; no new finite-group conjecture is posed.',reconstruction='The explicit normal-subgroup answer and enumeration method are expository annotations; the notebook only sets the exercise.',match='none found',verification='indirect',development='annotated',area='Finite group theory')
item(494,'P000925','question','Topology of SL(2,R) and its universal covering manifold',r'''Is \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\) simply connected? A quadratic change of coordinates gives \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\simeq S^1\times\mathbb R^2\).''',r'''The Iwasawa decomposition \(SL(2,\mathbb R)=KAN\), with \(K=SO(2)\), positive diagonal \(A\simeq\mathbb R\), and upper unipotent \(N\simeq\mathbb R\), is a diffeomorphism of manifolds. Hence \(\pi_1SL(2,\mathbb R)\simeq\mathbb Z\), and its universal cover is diffeomorphic to \(\mathbb R^3\). The product decomposition is not a direct-product decomposition of Lie groups.''','E',r'''This classical covering structure is used explicitly in \citet[\S4.2]{belgunmoroianu2025projective}; the notebook already supplies a direct quadratic-coordinate proof of the manifold decomposition.''','the question has the stated negative answer, with infinite cyclic fundamental group.',['belgunmoroianu2025projective'],literal='correct',area='Lie groups and homogeneous geometry')
item(494,'P000925-P000926','construction','The universal covering group and a triple-line cocycle',r'''Pairs \((g,n)\) are multiplied with a triple-line index correction. The display writes \(g\in SL(2,\mathbb C)\), then defines a phase using signs of real matrix entries; the kernel of a phase condition is said to have four components, from which the connected one is taken.''',r'''The universal covering group of \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\) can be defined by homotopy classes relative endpoints of paths from the identity, with pointwise path multiplication. Its kernel is \(\mathbb Z\). Choosing a section gives an integer cocycle \(c(g,h)\), and \((g,m)(h,n)=(gh,m+n+c(g,h))\). A triple-line index can express a cohomologous cocycle only after degenerate triples, normalization, coboundary and topology have been specified.''','E',r'''The lift-action description is classical and is made precise in \citet[\S4.2]{belgunmoroianu2025projective}. The path formulation reconstructs the intended covering group; it does not certify the notebook’s incomplete phase/cocycle normalization.''','the covering group is established. The literal SL(2,C), real sign formulas and component assertions do not define that group without correction.',['belgunmoroianu2025projective'],reconstruction='The path-group construction is supplied to separate the identified object from an unverified explicit cocycle.',literal='false as written',match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Lie groups and homogeneous geometry')
a=item(494,'P000926','problem','Countably many projective structures on the real line up to isomorphism',r'''Kirillov asks for all atlases on \(\mathbb R\) with fractional-linear transitions and says there are countably many structures. The affine line and coverings of the projective line are examples.''',r'''Up to projective diffeomorphism, connected open one-dimensional real projective manifolds have countably many isomorphism classes. A developing map identifies one with an interval in \(\widetilde{\mathbb RP^1}\simeq\mathbb R\). Up to lifted projective transformations and orientation reversal, the classes are the full cover, a half-line, and bounded intervals indexed by positive half-integral winding number. If equivalence instead means compatible atlases on a fixed underlying line, without quotienting by diffeomorphisms, the countability statement is false.''','E',r'''The classification is traced to Kuiper (1954) in \citet[Proposition 4.15 and Corollary 4.16]{belgunmoroianu2025projective}; that primary 2025 preprint explicitly recovers the countable open-curve classification while correcting separate closed-curve issues. This differs from the circle problem in 1976-403.6.''','the countability assertion is proved under projective-isomorphism equivalence; this is a resolved classification problem.',['belgunmoroianu2025projective'],problem='GP-1980-01',speaker='A. A. Kirillov',area='Projective geometry')
prob('GP-1980-01','Projective structures on the real line',r'''Classify connected real projective one-manifolds diffeomorphic to \(\mathbb R\), up to projective diffeomorphism.''',[a],r'''Resolved: the full universal projective line, a half-line, and the positive half-integral winding classes of bounded intervals; see \citet[Corollary 4.16]{belgunmoroianu2025projective}, which attributes the open-curve result to Kuiper (1954).''',['belgunmoroianu2025projective'])
corr('P000926','Covering-group pairs',r'\(g_j\in SL(2,\mathbb C)\), followed by signs of entries and a covering of \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\).',r'Read the intended base group as \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\); retain the unresolved normalization of the triple-line cocycle explicitly.','The scan clearly has C, while the preceding question, inequalities and real-line geometry all require R.','faithful_error')
corr('P000926','Countability and atlas equivalence','Countably many locally projective structures after defining equivalent atlases by compatibility.','Countability holds for isomorphism classes under projective diffeomorphisms, not equality of structures on a fixed line.','The scan’s compatibility definition and countability conclusion concern different equivalence levels unless a diffeomorphism quotient is supplied.','ambiguity')
save()

# Record 495.
talk(495,'1980-10-27','Bernard Gaveau','P000925-P000930','Levi-flat fillings and gauge-covariant stochastic constructions',r'''Gaveau’s heading begins near the bottom of P000926 and continues through P000928. The first topic concerns envelopes of holomorphy and disc fillings of spheres in complex dimension two; the second constructs gauge-covariant diffusion on connection spaces with a fixed heat-kernel cutoff; the final formulas concern Feynman--Kac, spectral growth and exponential moment estimates. His brief list of possible topics is not evidence that a full scattering theorem was stated. P000929--P000930 begin the dated 3 November session and are not part of this talk.''')
a=item(495,'P000926','problem','Levi-flat filling of an embedded two-sphere',r'''Find a Levi-flat three-dimensional manifold \(M\subset\mathbb C^2\) with \(\partial M=S^2\), foliated by complex curves. The drawing shows a one-parameter family of discs with two end points.''',r'''A precise proved version assumes a smooth embedded sphere in the boundary of a strictly pseudoconvex domain in \(\mathbb C^2\), exactly two elliptic complex points, and total reality elsewhere. It bounds a Levi-flat hypersurface foliated by analytic discs, smooth up to the sphere away from the elliptic points. The notebook does not state these hypotheses; arbitrary embedded two-spheres need not admit such a filling.''','A',r'''The closely matching speaker publication is Bedford--Gaveau (1983), identified in the primary account \citet[Introduction, p.~516]{gaussiersukhov2011filling}, which states its hypotheses and theorem. The same paper’s Theorem 1.1 gives a later almost-complex extension and notes counterexamples without pseudoconvexity. The 1983 original was identified bibliographically rather than read in full.''','the specified sphere theorem is established; a universal assertion for arbitrary embedded spheres is false.',['gaussiersukhov2011filling'],reconstruction='Strict pseudoconvexity and the two elliptic-point assumptions come from the later published theorem, not the manuscript.',match='partial',verification='indirect',problem='GP-1980-02',area='Several complex variables')
prob('GP-1980-02','Levi-flat filling of real two-spheres',r'''For a specified smooth embedded sphere \(S\subset\mathbb C^2\), determine whether it bounds a Levi-flat hypersurface foliated by analytic discs.''',[a],r'''The two-elliptic-point sphere in a strictly pseudoconvex boundary is fillable by Bedford--Gaveau (1983); arbitrary spheres are not universally fillable. \citet[Theorem 1.1 and Introduction]{gaussiersukhov2011filling} supplies a precise later extension and records the counterexamples. This entry does not assert an exhaustive classification of all embeddings.''',['gaussiersukhov2011filling'])
item(495,'P000926','claim','The asserted obstruction for a torus',r'''The parenthetical sentence says that this problem cannot be solved for a torus.''',r'''There is no obstruction based only on torus topology: \(M=\{(z,w):|z|\le1,\ |w|=1\}\subset\mathbb C^2\) is a smooth solid torus with boundary the Clifford torus. Its interior is Levi-flat and is foliated by the discs \(w=\mathrm{constant}\). A prohibition would need additional embedding, hull or boundary conditions absent from the notes.''','E',r'''The explicit product example is an elementary expository counterexample. The sphere-filling theorem in \citet[Introduction]{gaussiersukhov2011filling} does not imply a universal nonfilling theorem for tori.''','the literal torus assertion is false.',['gaussiersukhov2011filling'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',area='Several complex variables')
item(495,'P000927','claim','A degenerate boundary problem of index zero',r'''An isolated foliation singularity is said to lead to a degenerate Riemann problem of index zero, hence solvability; an integro-differential equation is mentioned.''',r'''Index zero alone states equality of kernel and cokernel dimensions for a Fredholm operator; it does not imply surjectivity. The notes do not specify the operator, domain, boundary regularity, ellipticity or a separate uniqueness argument, so no definite solvability theorem can be recovered.''','F','The adjacent Levi-flat discussion identifies the area, but the displayed implication has no recoverable hypotheses or operator. Searches for the Bedford--Gaveau filling work did not identify this exact analytic step.','this is an unrecoverable assertion as recorded; index zero without a kernel calculation is insufficient.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Several complex variables')
item(495,'P000927','method','Gauge-invariant functional integration as a regularized program',r'''The formal density is \(\exp(-\int\operatorname{Tr}(\Omega\Omega))D\omega\), and gauge transformations act by \(g^{-1}dg+g^{-1}\omega g\). A functional integral on trajectories is proposed.''',r'''For a principal compact-group bundle over a compact Riemannian manifold, the gauge action on connections is \(A^g=g^{-1}Ag+g^{-1}dg\), and the Yang--Mills energy is \(\int|F_A|^2\,d\mathrm{vol}\), using an invariant positive inner product. The symbol \(DA\) is formal. The identified construction replaces it by a stochastic process with fixed positive smoothing parameter; it is not a construction of a translation-invariant infinite-dimensional Lebesgue measure or of a cutoff-free four-dimensional Yang--Mills theory.''','A',r'''Gaveau--Trauber’s connection-space construction appeared in JFA 38 (1980), 324--341, after a 1979 C. R. announcement. The author’s primary follow-up \citet[Introduction and references 2,5]{gaveaumazet1981elliptic} describes that result and the separate 1980 cutoff quantization announcement. It explicitly acknowledges the Moscow and Leningrad autumn-1980 seminars.''','the regularized construction is identified; no claim about removing the cutoff follows from these notes.',['gaveautrauber1980diffusions','gaveaumazet1981elliptic'],match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Stochastic analysis and gauge theory')
item(495,'P000927','construction','Covariant Hodge heat kernels as diffusion covariance',r'''For a connection \(\Pi\), define \(\Box_\Pi=d_\Pi^*d_\Pi+d_\Pi d_\Pi^*\), use its heat kernel at fixed \(\tau\), and contract it with the second functional derivative of a functional.''',r'''On adjoint-bundle-valued one-forms over a compact Riemannian base, \(\Box_A\) is a nonnegative self-adjoint elliptic operator. For \(\tau>0\), \(Q_A=e^{-\tau\Box_A}\) is smoothing and trace class. For smooth cylindrical \(\Phi\), \(L\Phi(A)=\tfrac12\operatorname{Tr}(Q_A D^2\Phi(A))\) is a well-defined second-order expression. Gauge covariance of \(\Box_A\) implies covariance of its heat kernel and invariance of this expression.''','A',r'''The exact construction is Gaveau--Trauber (1980), described by the authors in \citet[pp.~105--106 and \S2]{gaveaumazet1981elliptic}; the latter explicitly says that the connection-space operator uses the heat kernel as its symbol.''','the fixed-cutoff cylindrical generator is established. Its stochastic realization needs the regularity and lifetime hypotheses considered separately.',['gaveautrauber1980diffusions','gaveaumazet1981elliptic'],match='exact',verification='indirect',area='Stochastic analysis and gauge theory')
item(495,'P000927-P000928','claim','Heat-kernel regularity and the stochastic differential equation',r'''The heat operators satisfy a Hilbert--Schmidt Lipschitz estimate in a \(C^{k,\alpha}\) connection norm. The notes write \(d\Pi_t=e^{-\tau\Box_\tau}d\beta_t\), using white noise; Sobolev spaces are said not to work.''',r'''For compact base, fixed positive \(\tau\), sufficient coefficient regularity, and a bounded neighborhood of connections, parabolic estimates give local Lipschitz dependence of smoothing heat operators in suitable operator norms. A local stochastic equation uses the current connection \(A_t\), and a noise coefficient \(e^{-\tau\Box_{A_t}/2}\) has covariance \(e^{-\tau\Box_{A_t}}\). With coefficient \(e^{-\tau\Box_{A_t}}\), the covariance is instead \(e^{-2\tau\Box_{A_t}}\); the It\^o generator has the factor \(1/2\). The construction does not imply that every possible Sobolev formulation is impossible.''','A',r'''The method is described in \citet[Introduction, \S3, Theorem 2 and its proof]{gaveaumazet1981elliptic}, referring back to Gaveau--Trauber (1980), \S\S2--3. The opened follow-up proves its analogous loop-space diffusion up to its lifetime, not an unconditional global theorem for the notebook equation.''','the regularized local construction is identified; global lifetime, the exact Banach-space norm and the cutoff normalization are not supplied by the manuscript.',['gaveautrauber1980diffusions','gaveaumazet1981elliptic'],literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',area='Stochastic analysis and gauge theory')
item(495,'P000928','theorem','Feynman--Kac representation and the bottom of the spectrum',r'''For \(\partial_t\psi=(\tfrac12\Delta-V)\psi\), the notes write a Brownian expectation and a large-time limit equal to \(\pm\lambda_1\).''',r'''For bounded real \(V\), Brownian motion with generator \(\tfrac12\Delta\) gives \((e^{-tH}f)(x)=\mathbb E_x[e^{-\int_0^tV(B_s)ds}f(B_t)]\), \(H=-\tfrac12\Delta+V\). On a connected compact manifold, the positive ground state implies \(\lim_{t\to\infty}t^{-1}\log\mathbb E_x e^{-\int_0^tV(B_s)ds}=-\lambda_0(H)\). Broader noncompact versions need their own potential and spectral hypotheses.''','E',r'''This is the classical Feynman--Kac formula and its ground-state consequence; a systematic primary treatment is \citet[\S\S A,B]{simon1982semigroups}. The sign is fixed by the convention for \(H\); the manuscript’s explicit plus-or-minus sign is not a convention-independent theorem.''','the formula and compact-case limit hold with the stated sign and hypotheses.',['simon1982semigroups'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',area='Spectral theory and probability')
item(495,'P000928','theorem','Khasminskii exponential bound and the missing negative part',r'''The displayed assumption is \(|\Delta^{-1}V|\le C<1\), followed by \(\mathbb E_x\exp(-\int_0^\infty V(B_s)ds)\le(1-C)^{-1}\).''',r'''For a Markov process and \(W\ge0\), if \(\sup_x\mathbb E_x\int_0^T W(B_s)ds\le C<1\), then \(\sup_x\mathbb E_x\exp(\int_0^TW(B_s)ds)\le(1-C)^{-1}\). The infinite-time version follows when the same occupation bound holds with \(T=\infty\). For the notebook’s sign take \(W=V_-\). A bound on the absolute value of the Green potential of signed \(V\) permits cancellations and is not this hypothesis; the Green operator must also match the Brownian normalization.''','E',r'''The estimate is the classical Khasminskii lemma; see \citet[\S B.1]{simon1982semigroups}. Expanding the exponential and applying the Markov property bounds its ordered-integral term of order \(n\) by \(C^n\), yielding the geometric series.''','the corrected bound is established. The signed Green-potential condition as written is insufficient.',['simon1982semigroups'],literal='false as written',development='annotated',area='Spectral theory and probability')
corr('P000926','Parenthetical torus assertion','This problem cannot be solved for a torus.','No topology-only obstruction exists: the product solid torus in C2 is Levi-flat and disc-foliated; further boundary or hull restrictions would be needed.','Scan inspected. The parenthetical claim is explicit, and the elementary product example refutes its unrestricted reading.')
corr('P000928','Stochastic equation (9)',r'\(d\Pi_t=e^{-\tau\Box_\tau}d\beta_t\).',r'The operator depends on \(\Pi_t\); distinguish the heat time in a noise coefficient from the heat time in its covariance, and retain the It\^o factor \(1/2\).','The scan has the tau subscript. Multiplying the heat operator by its adjoint doubles its time parameter.')
corr('P000928','Large-time formula (12)',r'\(\pm\lambda_1\).',r'For \(H=-\tfrac12\Delta+V\), the displayed logarithmic expectation limit is \(-\lambda_0(H)\), under the required spectral hypotheses.','Scan inspected: the plus-or-minus sign is genuine. The semigroup convention in (10) determines the negative sign.','ambiguity')
corr('P000928','Exponential estimate (13)--(14)',r'\(|\Delta^{-1}V|\le C<1\).',r'Use \(\sup_x\mathbb E_x\int_0^\infty V_-(B_s)ds\le C<1\), with the Green operator normalized to the process.','The scan contains an absolute value around the signed Green potential, not the negative-part potential. Khasminskii’s estimate needs the latter.')
save()

# Record 496. Its actual manuscript starts after the Gelfand history intervention.
talk(496,'1980-11-03','A. A. Beilinson','P000928-P000930','D-affinity and localization on the flag variety',r'''The Beilinson heading is on P000930, following Gelfand’s separately catalogued historical introduction on P000929. His talk continues through P000932, outside this record’s short catalogue range. It moves from affine schemes and quasi-coherent sheaves to D-affinity, induced modules, left/right operator issues and the Beilinson--Bernstein localization theorem at the trivial infinitesimal character. The uncertainty about the converse categorical criterion is resolved in his 10 November continuation, record 498.''')
item(496,'P000930','theorem','Affine objects and their rings of functions',r'''Affine varieties are uniquely determined by their function rings; the notes then identify a commutative ring with an affine algebraic variety.''',r'''Affine schemes are contravariantly equivalent to commutative unital rings via \(A\mapsto\operatorname{Spec}A\). Classical reduced affine varieties over \(\mathbb C\) correspond to finitely generated reduced \(\mathbb C\)-algebras (and to domains if irreducibility is part of the definition). An arbitrary commutative ring is not the coordinate ring of a classical complex affine variety.''','E',r'''The classical affine-variety and coherent-sheaf foundations are \citet[Chapter II]{serre1955coherent}; the notebook moves from that category to the later scheme formulation without marking the change.''','the ring equivalence is correct in the scheme category; finite generation and reducedness cannot be omitted in the classical variety formulation.',['serre1955coherent'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(496,'P000930','construction','Localization of modules and quasi-coherent sheaves',r'''For \(X=\operatorname{Spec}A\), the sheaf associated with \(M\) has \(\mathcal M(X_f)=M_f=M\otimes_A A_f\). The affine-open base-change condition is used as the definition of quasi-coherence.''',r'''For any \(A\)-module \(M\), the localization rule on principal opens defines a quasi-coherent sheaf \(\widetilde M\). Global sections and sheafification are inverse equivalences between \(A\)-modules and quasi-coherent sheaves on \(\operatorname{Spec}A\). For affine opens \(V\subset U\), a quasi-coherent sheaf satisfies \(\mathcal M(V)=\mathcal O(V)\otimes_{\mathcal O(U)}\mathcal M(U)\).''','E',r'''The coherent finite-type version is established in \citet[Chapter II, \S3]{serre1955coherent}; the arbitrary-module quasi-coherent version is its standard affine-scheme extension.''','the localization construction and equivalence are standard.',['serre1955coherent'],literal='correct',match='partial',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(496,'P000930','theorem','Serre’s cohomological criterion for affineness',r'''Affineness is characterized by vanishing of higher cohomology and generation of every quasi-coherent sheaf by global sections.''',r'''For a separated algebraic variety of finite type, affineness is equivalent to \(H^i(X,\mathcal F)=0\) for every quasi-coherent \(\mathcal F\) and \(i>0\). On an affine variety, the evaluation map \(\mathcal O_X\otimes_{\Gamma\mathcal O_X}\Gamma\mathcal F\to\mathcal F\) is also surjective. Thus the two conditions stated together in the notes are equivalent to affineness in this setting.''','E',r'''This is the affine-cohomology criterion introduced in \citet[Chapter II, \S3]{serre1955coherent}, with the quasi-coherent formulation standard in the scheme setting.''','the statement is established with the finite-type and separation hypotheses.',['serre1955coherent'],area='Algebraic geometry')
item(496,'P000930-P000931','definition','Quasi-coherent D-modules and D-affinity',r'''A D-module is called quasi-coherent if its underlying O-module is quasi-coherent. D-affinity requires higher-cohomology vanishing and generation by global sections.''',r'''For smooth \(X/\mathbb C\), let \(\mathcal D_X\) be finite-order algebraic differential operators and consider left D-modules quasi-coherent over \(\mathcal O_X\). D-affinity means that every such \(\mathcal M\) is acyclic for \(\Gamma\) and the evaluation \(\mathcal D_X\otimes_{\Gamma\mathcal D_X}\Gamma\mathcal M\to\mathcal M\) is surjective. The order of factors records the left-module convention.''','A',r'''This is the localization setting of \citet[pp.~15--18]{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}. Contemporary independent testimony in \citet[p.~44]{brylinski1981differential} explicitly records Beilinson--Bernstein’s stronger vanishing theorem for every quasi-coherent D-module. The November 1980 notes are an early seminar exposition.''','the definition is standard and supports the categorical equivalence in the sequel.',['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','brylinski1981differential'],area='Representation theory and D-modules')
item(496,'P000931','theorem','Affine varieties, projective spaces, and the elliptic counterexample',r'''Every affine variety and every projective space is D-affine; an elliptic curve is not.''',r'''A smooth affine complex variety is D-affine by ordinary affine quasi-coherent cohomology and generation. Complex \(\mathbb P^N\) is D-affine by localization (or its projective-space case). An elliptic curve \(E\) is not: \(\mathcal O_E\) is a left D-module and \(H^1(E,\mathcal O_E)\ne0\).''','A',r'''The projective assertion is a special case of \citet[pp.~15--18]{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}; its all-module vanishing is independently recorded in \citet[p.~44]{brylinski1981differential}. The affine and elliptic observations are elementary consequences of the definition and earlier coherent cohomology.''','all three examples have the stated outcomes; the positive-dimensional projective example is the new localization content.',['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','brylinski1981differential','serre1955coherent'],literal='correct',area='Representation theory and D-modules')
item(496,'P000931','construction','Induced D-modules from negative line bundles',r'''Even for \(\mathcal L=\mathcal O(-i)\) on projective space, \(\mathcal D_X\otimes_{\mathcal O_X}\mathcal L\) is generated by global sections and acyclic. The notes say \(\mathcal O(-i)\) itself has no sections and has higher cohomology.''',r'''For quasi-coherent \(\mathcal L\), induction uses the right O-action on \(\mathcal D_X\), producing a left D-module. On D-affine \(X\) it is acyclic and D-generated by its global sections. On \(\mathbb P^N\), \(H^0\mathcal O(-i)=0\) for \(i>0\), while nonzero higher cohomology occurs only for \(i\ge N+1\); for \(1\le i\le N\) all cohomology vanishes. For an invertible \(\mathcal L\), the induced module is locally free of rank one over D.''','A',r'''The induced-module consequence follows directly from the all-module localization theorem recorded in \citet[p.~44]{brylinski1981differential} and \citet{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}; the line-bundle cohomology is classical.''','the induced-module assertion holds, but the blanket higher-cohomology assertion for every negative twist does not.',['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','brylinski1981differential'],literal='false as written',area='Representation theory and D-modules')
item(496,'P000931-P000932','claim','Reversing the tensor product and formal transposition',r'''The reversed \(\mathcal L\otimes_{\mathcal O}\mathcal D\) has \(\mathcal L\) as a direct summand. An involution of D fixing functions would identify the two constructions. On an elliptic curve formal adjoint is written \(f\partial_x\mapsto\partial_xf\).''',r'''The reversed tensor is naturally a right D-module, with the left O-action on D used in forming it. As an O-module, evaluation \(P\mapsto P(1)\) splits \(\mathcal O\hookrightarrow\mathcal D\), so \(\mathcal L\) is a direct summand. Formal transposition reverses order and sends \(\partial_x\mapsto-\partial_x\) in a constant-volume coordinate; globally it exchanges left and right modules via the canonical bundle. It is not an unrestricted canonical involution of the same untwisted ring.''','E',r'''This is the classical left/right duality of differential operators used in \citet[Introduction, formulae for duality]{kashiwara1984riemann}. The source’s missing minus sign already contradicts \([\partial_x,x]=1\).''','the splitting is correct; the side convention, canonical-bundle twist and adjoint sign are essential.',['kashiwara1984riemann'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',area='D-modules and algebraic geometry')
a=item(496,'P000932','question','Does equivalence by global sections imply D-affinity?',r'''D-affinity implies that \(\Gamma:\mathcal D_X\text{-mod}\to\Gamma(\mathcal D_X)\text{-mod}\) is an equivalence. The speaker says he does not know the converse.''',r'''For the category of O-quasi-coherent left D-modules on a smooth separated finite-type variety, D-affinity is equivalent to the natural global-sections/localization adjunction being an equivalence. In the reverse implication, equivalence gives exactness and detection of nonzero objects; an affine \v{C}ech resolution computes the higher sheaf cohomology.''','A',r'''The notebook itself supplies the converse one week later, in record 498, by the compact projective generator argument. This abstract mechanism is the standard module-category criterion, stated with proof outline in \citet[\S1.2]{keller1997categories}; the localization application is \citet{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}.''','the question is answered affirmatively under these hypotheses, including in the next recorded talk.',['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','keller1997categories'],problem='GP-1980-03',area='Representation theory and D-modules')
prob('GP-1980-03','Categorical characterization of D-affinity',r'''For a smooth separated finite-type complex variety, does equivalence of the natural global-sections/localization adjunction for quasi-coherent left D-modules imply vanishing of all higher sheaf cohomology and generation by global sections?''',[a],r'''Yes. Beilinson’s 10 November continuation, 1980-498.2--3, gives the projective-generator and \v{C}ech argument. This is a resolved question, not a present open problem.''',['keller1997categories','beilinsonbernstein1981localisation'])
item(496,'P000932','claim','The canonical class obstruction and the limits of Chern-class arguments',r'''Vanishing of the first Chern class of the canonical or tangent bundle is said to imply non-D-affinity and existence of an involution. An insertion identifies line-bundle isomorphism with equality of first Chern classes.''',r'''Without properness the non-D-affinity claim is false: affine space has trivial canonical bundle and is D-affine. A safe precise special case is that a smooth connected projective variety of positive dimension with trivial canonical bundle is not D-affine, since \(H^{\dim X}(X,\mathcal O_X)\simeq\mathbb C\) by Serre duality. Equality of first Chern classes does not imply algebraic or holomorphic isomorphism of line bundles: nontrivial degree-zero line bundles on an elliptic curve have the same Chern class as \(\mathcal O\).''','E',r'''The obstruction is an elementary application of coherent duality; the global transpose naturally involves the canonical bundle, as in \citet[Introduction]{kashiwara1984riemann}. The source does not specify whether its Chern class is integral, rational, de Rham or an Atiyah class, so the stronger claimed equivalence is not certified.''','the unqualified implications are false. The stated projective trivial-canonical case is established; it is not claimed to exhaust the intended theorem.',['kashiwara1984riemann'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',confidence='medium',area='D-modules and algebraic geometry')
item(496,'P000932','theorem','Global operators on the flag variety and localization',r'''For semisimple \(G\) and Borel \(B\), \(X=G/B\) is D-affine and \(\Gamma\mathcal D_X=U(\mathfrak g)/U(\mathfrak g)I_0\), where \(I_0\) is the kernel of the trivial central character. Hence D-modules correspond to representations with that infinitesimal character.''',r'''For a connected complex semisimple algebraic group \(G\), put \(X=G/B\) and let \(\chi_{\rm triv}:Z(U\mathfrak g)\to\mathbb C\) be the character on the trivial representation. The infinitesimal action induces \(U(\mathfrak g)/U(\mathfrak g)\ker\chi_{\rm triv}\simeq\Gamma(X,\mathcal D_X)\). Global sections and localization give inverse equivalences between O-quasi-coherent left \(\mathcal D_X\)-modules and modules over this central reduction. This uses the exact central character, not merely generalized central-character torsion.''','A',r'''\citet[pp.~15--18]{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation} is the direct speaker publication (1981; seminar exposition November 1980). \citet[theorem on p.~43 and addendum p.~44]{brylinski1981differential}, from the August--September 1980 Toru\'n conference, independently proves the global-ring assertion and records the Beilinson--Bernstein all-module vanishing.''','the untwisted localization theorem is established. Its regular dominant character convention is fixed here by the trivial representation.',['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','brylinski1981differential'],literal='correct',area='Representation theory and D-modules')
item(496,'P000932','construction','The projective-line operator algebra and its central relation',r'''On \(\mathbb P^1\) the operators \(\partial_x,x\partial_x,x^2\partial_x\) generate the algebra; the notes say there is a unique operator commuting with all of them.''',r'''The three vector fields span \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\). Their universal enveloping algebra maps onto \(\Gamma(\mathbb P^1,\mathcal D)\), with kernel generated by the Casimir minus its trivial-character scalar. The center of \(U(\mathfrak{sl}_2)\) is a polynomial algebra in one Casimir; this means one algebraically independent generator, not one commuting operator. In the global differential-operator quotient the center consists of scalar operators.''','E',r'''The rank-one example already occurs in 1967-144.13. The general quotient description is proved in \citet[p.~43]{brylinski1981differential}; the Harish--Chandra center statement is recalled on p.~45. The generation and relation can also be checked directly in one variable.''','the example is classical; uniqueness must be read as uniqueness of a central generator up to normalization, not literally a single commuting operator.',['brylinski1981differential'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Representation theory and D-modules')
corr('P000930','Commutative ring equals affine algebraic variety','A commutative ring is an affine algebraic variety.','Use affine scheme for arbitrary rings; classical complex affine varieties require finitely generated reduced complex algebras.','The manuscript explicitly moves from varieties to Spec of an unrestricted ring.')
corr('P000931','Negative-twist cohomology (13)',r'Every \(\mathcal O(-i)\) on \(\mathbb P^N\) has higher cohomology.',r'For \(i>0\), nonzero higher cohomology occurs only when \(i\ge N+1\); twists \(1\le i\le N\) are acyclic.','Scan inspected: the symbol is i and no range beyond negativity is stated.')
corr('P000931','Formal adjoint (16)',r'\(f\partial_x\mapsto\partial_x f\).',r'\((f\partial_x)^*=-\partial_x f\) for the usual formal transpose against constant volume.','Scan inspected: no minus sign is visible. The Weyl commutation relation forces the sign for an anti-involution fixing functions.')
corr('P000932','Canonical-class obstruction (20)--(22)','Vanishing first Chern class implies non-D-affinity, without properness assumptions.','Supply an appropriate projective/compact hypothesis and specify which characteristic class; affine space refutes the unrestricted statement.','The scan has no projectivity hypothesis at this point; the prior definition explicitly includes affine varieties.')
corr('P000932','Interlinear line-bundle assertion','Invertible sheaves are isomorphic if and only if their first Chern classes agree.','Equal first Chern classes need not give holomorphically or algebraically isomorphic line bundles.','The interlinear assertion is visible; nontrivial Picard-zero line bundles on an elliptic curve give counterexamples.')
corr('P000932','Last projective-line sentence','There is a unique operator commuting with all three vector fields.','The enveloping algebra has one polynomial central generator; after the trivial-character quotient the commuting center is the scalar field.','The source’s uniqueness wording is explicit and cannot hold literally, since every scalar commutes.')
save()

# Record 497. This is the P929 introduction, not the overlapping Beilinson pages.
talk(497,'1980-11-03','I. M. Gelfand','P000928-P000934','Representation theory, noncommutative birational geometry, and sheaves',r'''The actual intervention occupies P000929, headed I.M.G. and History of representation theory. Its historical list runs through characters, Haar integration, operator algebras and Lorentz-group representations; these names are contextual remarks, not newly stated theorems or a reliable exclusive priority list. The mathematical content concerns enveloping algebras, the Gelfand--Kirillov birational conjecture, a poorly specified extension beyond polynomial coefficients, and the BGG sheaf method on projective space. The subsequent Beilinson heading on P000930 starts record 496. The Gelfand--Kirillov problem is the earlier GP-1967-21, not a new 1980 problem.''')
item(497,'P000929','method','Enveloping algebras and their quotient division rings',r'''The analogue of the group algebra for a Lie algebra is its enveloping algebra; there are many such algebras, and no Wedderburn theorem is available in the intended sense.''',r'''For a finite-dimensional Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\) over a characteristic-zero field, \(U(\mathfrak g)\) is a noetherian domain and has an Ore quotient division ring. The usual Wedderburn structure theorem for finite-dimensional semisimple associative algebras does not classify these infinite-dimensional algebras. Comparing their quotient division rings is a noncommutative birational classification problem.''','E',r'''This is the framework of \citet[Introduction, pp.~5--6]{gelfandkirillov1966corps}, already discussed in 1967-144.5--6. The statement that there is no Wedderburn theorem is interpreted as a limitation of that finite-dimensional theorem, not a claim that no structure theorem can exist.''','the Ore construction and birational formulation are established.',['gelfandkirillov1966corps'],reconstruction='The finite-dimensional and characteristic-zero hypotheses and the intended meaning of the Wedderburn comparison are supplied.',area='Noncommutative algebra')
a=item(497,'P000929','conjecture','The unextended Gelfand--Kirillov conjecture revisited',r'''The skew field of fractions of an enveloping algebra is identified with that of a ring of differential operators; Gelfand calls this unfinished.''',r'''For a finite-dimensional algebraic Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\) over an algebraically closed field \(k\) of characteristic zero, the original conjecture asks whether \(\operatorname{Frac}U(\mathfrak g)\simeq\operatorname{Frac}A_m(k(t_1,\ldots,t_r))\) for some \(m,r\ge0\), where \(A_m\) is a Weyl algebra and the \(t_i\) are central algebraically independent variables. An isomorphism after a finite extension of the center is a different, weaker assertion.''','B',r'''The exact recurrence is GP-1967-21 (1967-144.7); see \citet[Introduction]{gelfandkirillov1966corps}. The finite-central-extension result of \citet{gelfandkirillov1969structure} must be kept separate. \citet[Theorem~1]{premet2010modular} excludes simple types \(B_n\) for \(n\ge3\), \(D_n\) for \(n\ge4\), \(E_6,E_7,E_8,F_4\). Type A is positive. \citet[Introduction, pp.~2--3 of the author version]{marianoschwarz2024gelfand} still lists types C and G as unresolved; its version of record appeared in August 2025.''','the unrestricted conjecture is false. The checked literature through the present search leaves the simple cases \(C_n\), \(n\ge2\), and \(G_2\) open; the necessary-type restriction A, C, G is not a sufficiency theorem. No claimed 2026 resolution of these enveloping-algebra cases was found.',['gelfandkirillov1966corps','gelfandkirillov1969structure','premet2010modular','marianoschwarz2024gelfand'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1967-21',area='Noncommutative algebra')
prob('GP-1967-21','Gelfand--Kirillov birational conjecture: 1980 recurrence',r'''For an algebraic finite-dimensional Lie algebra over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, is its enveloping quotient division ring a Weyl division ring over a purely transcendental central field?''',[a],r'''The general statement is false; type A is established, and the reviewed literature still leaves simple types \(C_n\), \(n\ge2\), and \(G_2\) unresolved. Reuse the existing problem identity GP-1967-21; this row supplies the additional occurrence, not a replacement of earlier occurrences. A finite central extension changes the question.''',['gelfandkirillov1966corps','premet2010modular','marianoschwarz2024gelfand'])
item(497,'P000929','program','Differential operators beyond polynomial coefficients',r'''Polynomial coefficients are said to be insufficient, with partial understanding attributed to Novikov, Krichever and Shabat; an additional name is cancelled.''',r'''No class of coefficient functions, differential operators, equivalence relation, or proposed replacement for the Weyl division field is specified. The named researchers suggest the theory of commuting differential operators and integrable systems, but the sentence does not determine a theorem or conjecture in that theory.''','F','The names and the neighboring birational statement are recoverable; a precise publication match cannot be inferred from them. The statement is retained as an identifiable research direction without assigning it an invented finite-gap or spectral-curve theorem.','the intended mathematical assertion remains unrecoverable from this source.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Integrable systems and differential operators')
item(497,'P000929','method','The BGG sheaf approach on projective space',r'''Bernstein, S. Gelfand and I.M. Gelfand are named in connection with projective space, followed by the instruction to work with sheaves.''',r'''The identifiable construction associates to a finite-dimensional graded module over the exterior algebra on an \((n+1)\)-dimensional space a linear complex of vector bundles on \(\mathbb P^n\). In the BGG theorem, the stable category modulo graded free modules is equivalent to \(D^b(\operatorname{Coh}\mathbb P^n)\). This precise statement identifies the named method from its publication; the notebook itself states no categorical equivalence.''','E',r'''\citet[Theorems~1--2, pp.~212--213]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1978bundles}, submitted 10 January 1978, is the exact author/subject identification. It is also discussed in the previous companion’s records 1977-437 and 1977-438. The November 1980 sentence is a retrospective pointer.''','the construction and equivalence are established. The short pointer cannot be used as evidence that every detail of the published theorem was stated in this intervention.',['bernsteingelfandgelfand1978bundles'],reconstruction='The exterior-algebra and derived-category formulation comes from the identified paper, not from the notebook sentence.',match='partial',area='Algebraic geometry and representation theory')
save()

# Record 498: source begins below Gelfand on P933 and continues through P936.
talk(498,'1980-11-10','A. A. Beilinson','P000932-P000934','A-affinity, projective generators, localization and transfer bimodules',r'''The actual heading is in the lower part of P000933; the talk continues through P000936. It proves the categorical affinity criterion left uncertain on 3 November, formulates the compact projective generator lemma, repeats projective-space and flag-variety localization, and begins the transfer-bimodule construction. The preceding Cayley-algebra intervention on the same page is record 499. The short catalogue range stops before most of the proof and must not determine the content boundary.''')
item(498,'P000933-P000934','construction','Global sections and localization for a sheaf of rings',r'''Let \(\mathcal A\) contain \(\mathcal O_X\) and be quasi-coherent over it. For \(A=\Gamma(X,\mathcal A)\), the functors are \(\Gamma\) and \(\Delta(M)=\mathcal A\otimes_A M\); the source calls the constant sheaf of M a sheaf of \(\mathcal A\)-modules.''',r'''Let X be a separated finite-type complex variety and \(\mathcal A\) a sheaf of unital rings with a given inclusion of \(\mathcal O_X\), quasi-coherent for its left O-action. In the category of left A-sheaves whose underlying O-module is quasi-coherent, localization \(\Delta(M)=\mathcal A\otimes_{\underline A}\underline M\) is left adjoint to global sections. Here \(\underline A\) and \(\underline M\) are constant sheaves, with the natural map \(\underline A\to\mathcal A\). The O-subring need not be central; differential operators are the motivating example.''','E',r'''This is the standard tensor--Hom adjunction used by the projective-generator proof, cf. \citet[\S1.2]{keller1997categories}. It generalizes the affine sheaf/module construction of \citet[Chapter II, \S3]{serre1955coherent}.''','the adjunction is valid. A general left A-module does not already carry a canonical constant-sheaf action by the varying ring \(\mathcal A\).',['keller1997categories','serre1955coherent'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Algebraic geometry and category theory')
a=item(498,'P000934-P000935','theorem','Cohomological affinity and equivalence of module categories',r'''Two conditions are stated to be equivalent: every quasi-coherent A-module has no higher cohomology and every nonzero one has a nonzero global section; or \(\Gamma,\Delta\) are inverse equivalences. The reverse direction is assigned to the \v{C}ech resolution.''',r'''For X and \(\mathcal A\) as in the preceding item, assume the category is closed under the indicated quasi-coherent operations. Vanishing of \(H^{i>0}(X,\mathcal M)\) for every object and detection of nonzero objects by \(\Gamma\) are equivalent to the natural localization/global-sections adjunction being an equivalence. They also imply D-style generation by global sections: the cokernel of the evaluation map is zero. For the converse, use an affine \v{C}ech resolution whose terms and kernels remain in the category.''','A',r'''This explicitly resolves 1980-496.8 and GP-1980-03 in the source itself. The general category-theoretic mechanism is \citet[\S1.2]{keller1997categories}; the D-module application is the setting of \citet{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}.''','the criterion is established under the stated quasi-coherent and finite-cover hypotheses. Detection of nonzero objects and exactness, taken together, suffice to recover the generation condition.',['keller1997categories','beilinsonbernstein1981localisation'],problem='GP-1980-03',area='Representation theory and D-modules')
b=item(498,'P000934-P000935','lemma','A compact projective generator identifies an abelian category with modules',r'''An abelian category has arbitrary direct sums and a projective generator P with \(\operatorname{Hom}(P,-)\) preserving them. The notes identify its target as left \(\operatorname{End}(P)\)-modules and write \(\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal A}(\mathcal A)=A\).''',r'''For a locally small abelian category with arbitrary coproducts and a compact projective generator P, \(\operatorname{Hom}(P,-)\) is an equivalence to right modules over \(\operatorname{End}(P)\), or left modules over its opposite ring. Compact means preservation of arbitrary coproducts. For left \(\mathcal A\)-modules, \(\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal A}(\mathcal A)\simeq\Gamma(X,\mathcal A)^{\mathrm{op}}\), so the resulting target is indeed left A-modules. On a quasi-compact separated variety, global sections of quasi-coherent sheaves commute with coproducts.''','E',r'''This classical module-category criterion is stated with its historical attribution and proof outline in \citet[\S1.2, p.~4]{keller1997categories}. The proof takes a presentation by coproducts of P and applies its exact Hom functor. The source has the compactness hypothesis but omits the opposite-ring convention.''','the lemma is established. The missing opposite ring is a literal error under the explicitly chosen left-module convention.',['keller1997categories'],literal='false as written',problem='GP-1980-03',development='annotated',area='Category theory')
for p in P:
 if p['id']=='GP-1980-03':p['occurrences'].extend([a,b])
item(498,'P000935','theorem','Projective space is D-affine',r'''Theorem (13): \(\mathbb P^N\) is D-affine.''',r'''For complex projective space and the sheaf of finite-order algebraic differential operators, every O-quasi-coherent left D-module is acyclic for global sections and is generated as a D-module by them. Consequently the localization/global-sections adjunction is an equivalence.''','A',r'''This repeats the projective-space assertion in 1980-496.5. It follows from the localization theorem of \citet{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}; the all-module vanishing is explicitly recorded in \citet[p.~44]{brylinski1981differential}.''','the theorem is established; the source means algebraic D-affinity, not ordinary affineness of projective space.',['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','brylinski1981differential'],literal='correct',area='Representation theory and D-modules')
item(498,'P000935','theorem','Flag-variety localization for a reductive group',r'''For a reductive group G and Borel B, the Lie action gives \(U(\mathfrak g)\to\Gamma\mathcal D_{G/B}\); quotienting by the central annihilator of the trivial representation gives an isomorphism and the expected category equivalence.''',r'''For a connected complex reductive algebraic group G, let \(X=G/B\) and let \(I\subset Z(U\mathfrak g)\) be the kernel of the trivial central character. Then X is D-affine and \(U(\mathfrak g)/U(\mathfrak g)I\simeq\Gamma(X,\mathcal D_X)\). Thus O-quasi-coherent left D-modules correspond to all left modules over that central reduction. The central Lie algebra of G acts trivially on X and is killed by I; the reductive formulation reduces to the semisimple one.''','A',r'''The exact theorem is \citet[pp.~15--18]{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}; compare \citet[p.~43 and addendum p.~44]{brylinski1981differential}. This is a substantive second occurrence of 1980-496.10, now preceded by the categorical proof.''','the theorem is established with connectedness and the exact trivial infinitesimal character specified.',['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','brylinski1981differential'],area='Representation theory and D-modules')
item(498,'P000936','construction','The transfer bimodule and its two commuting operator actions',r'''The source defines \(\mathcal D_{X\to Y}=\mathcal O_X\otimes_{f^{-1}\mathcal O_Y}f^{-1}\mathcal D_Y\), with a left \(D_X\) action by Leibniz and a right pulled-back \(D_Y\) action. The last sentence says df(v) acts on \(f^{-1}\mathcal D_Y\) itself.''',r'''For a morphism f of smooth complex algebraic varieties, the displayed tensor is a \((\mathcal D_X,f^{-1}\mathcal D_Y)\)-bimodule. Locally, if \(df(v)=\sum_i a_i\otimes w_i\), then \[v\cdot(b\otimes P)=v(b)\otimes P+\sum_i ba_i\otimes w_iP.\] The right action is multiplication on P. The coefficients \(a_i\) are functions on X, so the formula acts on the tensor product, not generally on the unextended inverse-image sheaf alone.''','E',r'''The transfer-module formula is standard D-module functoriality; see \citet[\S4.3, p.~343]{kashiwara1984riemann}. The notebook captures the correct bimodule but abbreviates the vector-field action too far.''','the construction is established; \(O_X\) scalar extension is essential to the claimed left action.',['kashiwara1984riemann'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',area='D-modules and algebraic geometry')
item(498,'P000936','method','Derived direct and inverse image in place of a ring pullback',r'''There is no corresponding morphism of rings of differential operators for a general map, but natural functors exist in derived categories; the notes say there are four.''',r'''For a morphism of smooth varieties, differential operators do not pull back by a functorial homomorphism of sheaves of rings in the way functions do. Derived inverse and direct image use transfer bimodules and derived tensor products, with the canonical-bundle twist for the reverse transfer module. On the appropriate holonomic derived categories, duality produces the complementary extraordinary operations. Preservation of regularity and comparison with constructible sheaves require the regular-holonomic hypotheses.''','E',r'''These operations are part of the holonomic-system formalism in \citet[\S4.3 and \S8]{kashiwara1984riemann}; the regular derived correspondence was already announced in \citet[p.~XIX.1]{kashiwara1980constructible}. The source does not define four functors on every unbounded module category.''','the derived functoriality is established, with category and duality restrictions; the notebook’s count of four is a programmatic abbreviation.',['kashiwara1984riemann','kashiwara1980constructible'],match='partial',area='D-modules and algebraic geometry')
corr('P000934','Constant sheaf in localization (2)',r'M is a constant sheaf of \(\mathcal A\)-modules.',r'M defines a constant sheaf of modules over \(\underline A\), where \(A=\Gamma\mathcal A\); extension of scalars gives the \(\mathcal A\)-module.','Scan inspected: the module-ring symbol is calligraphic A. A general A-module has no pre-existing action by local sections of the sheaf ring.')
corr('P000935','Endomorphism ring (11)--(12)',r'Left \(\operatorname{End}(P)\)-modules and \(\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal A}(\mathcal A)=A\).',r'Use right End(P)-modules, or left modules over its opposite; for a left regular module the endomorphism ring is \(A^{\mathrm{op}}\).','The left-module convention is explicit on P933--934, while the scan of (12) has no opposite-ring symbol.')
corr('P000936','Last line after (25)',r'\(df(v)\) acts on \(f^{-1}\mathcal D_Y\).',r'The coefficients of df(v) lie in O_X; the induced action is on the scalar-extended transfer module.','The scan double-underlines the inverse-image expression without the O_X tensor factor. The preceding correct formula (24) supplies the needed module.','ambiguity')
save()

# Record 499 precedes the Beilinson portion on the same dated sheet.
talk(499,'1980-11-10','I. M. Gelfand; S. G. Gindikin intervention','P000932-P000938','Cayley numbers, absolute-valued algebras, and projective planes',r'''The actual intervention is the upper part of P000933, before Beilinson’s heading. Its questions concern multiplicative norms, the finite-dimensional restriction, a possible infinite-dimensional example, projective-plane coordinatization and a geometric approach to exceptional Lie theory. The notebook does not state a unit axiom. That omission changes both the finite classification and the answer to the infinite-dimensional question, and is recorded explicitly. No content from Beilinson’s following pages is assigned to this record.''')
item(499,'P000933','definition','The Cayley norm and multiplicative composition',r'''In a basis of eight Cayley units, \(\lvert\sum x_je_j\rvert^2=\sum x_j^2\), and \(|ab|=|a||b|\).''',r'''The real octonion algebra \(\mathbb O\) is an eight-dimensional unital, nonassociative alternative algebra. Conjugation gives \(N(a)=a\bar a=\sum_{j=1}^8x_j^2\), and \(N(ab)=N(a)N(b)\). Thus its Euclidean norm is multiplicative.''','E',r'''This is the classical Cayley composition algebra; see \citet[\S\S2.1--2.2]{baez2002octonions}. The note asks for a review of its classification, not a newly announced composition law.''','the norm identity is correct.',['baez2002octonions'],literal='correct',area='Nonassociative algebra')
item(499,'P000933','theorem','Dimensions of finite-dimensional absolute-valued algebras',r'''There are none of dimension greater than eight, and in finite dimensions only the real numbers, complex numbers, quaternions and Cayley numbers occur.''',r'''A nonzero finite-dimensional real algebra with a bilinear product and a multiplicative norm has dimension \(1,2,4\), or \(8\). If it has a two-sided identity, it is isometrically isomorphic to \(\mathbb R,\mathbb C,\mathbb H\), or \(\mathbb O\). Without an identity the dimension assertion remains true, but there are additional isotopes, so the four-algebra isomorphism list is incomplete.''','E',r'''The unital composition classification and its representation-theoretic proof are reviewed in \citet[Theorem~1 and \S2.3]{baez2002octonions}. The reduction of a finite-dimensional absolute-valued algebra to a unital isotope is explicitly proved before Proposition~1 in \citet[pp.~1--2]{kaidiramirezrodriguez1998absolute}.''','the dimension restriction is established. The unqualified isomorphism classification needs the missing unit hypothesis.',['baez2002octonions','kaidiramirezrodriguez1998absolute'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Nonassociative algebra')
item(499,'P000933','method','The Clifford-algebra route to the dimension restriction',r'''The norm identity gives a representation-theoretic problem and an explicit construction.''',r'''For a unital finite-dimensional Euclidean composition algebra A, left multiplication by vectors orthogonal to 1 satisfies \(L_uL_v+L_vL_u=-2\langle u,v\rangle\operatorname{Id}\). Thus A is a module for the real Clifford algebra of its imaginary subspace. Comparing possible module dimensions forces \(\dim A\in\{1,2,4,8\}\); the four usual algebras realize these possibilities. For a nonunital finite-dimensional algebra, pass first to a unital isotope.''','E',r'''The explicit representation-theoretic proof is \citet[\S2.3]{baez2002octonions}; the identity is obtained by polarizing the multiplicative Euclidean norm. This is an identification and expansion of the source’s one-line method, not a newly supplied classification proof.''','the Clifford argument is established and explains the speaker’s reference to representation theory.',['baez2002octonions'],reconstruction='The Clifford action and dimension comparison are supplied from the cited account; the source names neither the algebra nor the precise relation.',area='Nonassociative algebra and representation theory')
a=item(499,'P000933','question','Can a multiplicative norm exist in infinite dimension?',r'''Gelfand asks whether the multiplicative-norm identity can hold in infinite dimension; Gindikin says yes.''',r'''Without a unit, yes: take \(H=\ell^2(\mathbb N,\mathbb R)\), choose a bijection \(b:\mathbb N^2\to\mathbb N\), and define \((x*y)_{b(i,j)}=x_iy_j\). This is a continuous bilinear product with \(\|x*y\|^2=\sum_{i,j}|x_i|^2|y_j|^2=\|x\|^2\|y\|^2\). It has no two-sided identity: under the induced unitary \(H\mathbin{\widehat\otimes}_2H\to H\), the range of multiplication by a nonzero fixed x is the proper subspace corresponding to \(\mathbb Rx\otimes H\). With a two-sided identity, no infinite-dimensional example exists: the Urbanik--Wright theorem gives precisely \(\mathbb R,\mathbb C,\mathbb H,\mathbb O\), without assuming finite dimension or completeness.''','E',r'''The Urbanik--Wright theorem (1960) is stated explicitly in \citet[p.~2, preceding Corollary~1]{kaidiramirezrodriguez1998absolute}. The tensor-coordinate example is an elementary expository construction; it is not asserted to be Gindikin’s unpublished argument. The notebook omits the axiom separating the yes and no answers.''','the question is resolved under either explicit convention. Gindikin’s affirmative answer is consistent with the nonunital reading and inconsistent with the unital one.',['kaidiramirezrodriguez1998absolute'],reconstruction='The explicit Hilbert-space example is editorial and is not attributed historically to Gindikin.',literal='not determinable',match='partial',development='annotated',problem='GP-1980-04',area='Nonassociative functional analysis')
prob('GP-1980-04','Infinite-dimensional algebras with a multiplicative norm',r'''Does a real infinite-dimensional normed vector space admit a continuous bilinear multiplication satisfying \(\|xy\|=\|x\|\|y\|\)? Does the answer change when a two-sided identity is required?''',[a],r'''Yes without a unit, by the Hilbert tensor-coordinate construction; no with a unit, by Urbanik--Wright (1960). The notebook does not choose the unit convention, so this is a resolved disambiguation rather than a currently open problem.''',['kaidiramirezrodriguez1998absolute'])
item(499,'P000933','theorem','Associativity, Desargues, and the Cayley projective plane',r'''After coordinatization, associativity is identified with Desargues; the Cayley projective plane is called the simplest non-Desarguesian example.''',r'''For projective planes coordinatized by an alternative division algebra, the plane is Desarguesian precisely when that algebra is associative. The octonionic Moufang plane \(\mathbb OP^2\) is therefore non-Desarguesian. For an arbitrary projective plane, coordinatization uses a planar ternary ring and the bare word associativity is not a complete formulation. Calling the Cayley plane simplest is qualitative, not a minimality theorem.''','E',r'''The construction of \(\mathbb OP^2\), its incidence geometry and its relation to the failure of associativity are reviewed in \citet[\S3]{baez2002octonions}.''','the division-algebra formulation is established; the source’s shorthand requires the coordinatization category.',['baez2002octonions'],area='Projective geometry and nonassociative algebra')
item(499,'P000933','program','Differential and locally homogeneous projective planes',r'''It is interesting to study differential and locally homogeneous projective planes.''',r'''One could impose smooth point and line manifolds with smooth incidence and join/intersection maps, then ask for transitive or locally transitive collineation groups. The notes do not fix these regularity axioms, the dimension, completeness, the acting group or an equivalence relation, so they specify no unique classification question.''','F','The program is retained because its geometric object is identifiable, but no precise assertion can be reconstructed from this single sentence. The modern study of smooth projective planes does not by itself answer an unspecified classification request.','the source remains underdetermined; no new open problem or claim of nondevelopment is inferred.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Projective geometry')
item(499,'P000933','program','A unified geometric explanation of exceptional Lie types',r'''Classifying Lie algebras is called too particular; the exceptional types \(G_2,E_6,E_7,E_8\) should not appear as isolated fragments.''',r'''The request is for a conceptual geometric organization of exceptional Lie theory. Classical octonionic and Jordan-algebra constructions do provide relationships among exceptional groups, but the notebook gives no proposed object, theorem or classification target whose completion can be assessed.''','F',r'''\citet[\S4]{baez2002octonions} supplies a precise later account of octonionic constructions of exceptional Lie algebras. It is a thematic comparison, not evidence for a uniquely recoverable theorem in this source.''','the mathematical motivation is clear, but an exact assertion is not recoverable. This item is not classified as an undeveloped conjecture.',['baez2002octonions'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='low',area='Exceptional Lie theory')
corr('P000933','Finite and infinite absolute-valued algebras','Only R, C, H and Cayley in finite dimensions; an infinite-dimensional example exists.','Distinguish the unit axiom. Unital algebras are precisely R, C, H, O even without a dimension hypothesis; nonunital algebras admit infinite-dimensional examples and extra finite-dimensional isotopes.','Scan inspected: only the multiplicative norm condition is written, with no identity or division-surjectivity axiom. The ambiguity changes the mathematical answer.','ambiguity')
save()

